The du command in Linux/Unix stands for "Disk Usage". It is used to estimate and display the disk space used by files and directories. Below is a detailed explanation of its usage and common options.

<du [OPTIONS] [FILE/DIRECTORY]>

If no file/directory is specified, du reports the disk usage of the current directory.

Common Options
Option	             Description
-a	                 Show sizes for all files (not just directories)
-h	                 Print sizes in human-readable format (KB, MB, GB)
-s	                 Display only a summary (total size) for each argument
--max-depth=N	     Limit output to directories up to N levels deep
-c	                 Show a grand total at the end
--apparent-size	     Show apparent size (what ls shows) instead of disk usage

Basic Usage (Show directory sizes)

du /path/to/dir

Displays sizes in kilobytes (KB) for all subdirectories.

Show Human-Readable Sizes (MB, GB)

du -h /path/to/dir

Outputs sizes in KB, MB, or GB (e.g., 4.5M, 1.2G).

Show Sizes for All Files (Including Hidden Ones)

du -ah /path/to/dir

Show Only the Total Size of a Directory

du -sh /path/to/dir

Useful for checking total space used by a directory.

Limit Depth of Subdirectories Shown

du -h --max-depth=1 /path/to/dir

Shows sizes only for immediate subdirectories.

Find Largest Files/Directories (Combined with sort & head)

du -ah /path/to/dir | sort -rh | head -n 10

Lists the top 10 largest files/dirs in human-readable format.




By default, du reports disk usage, not just file size (accounts for filesystem block allocation).
Use -h for easier-to-read output.
Combine with sort -h to properly sort human-readable sizes.
For apparent size (like ls -l), use --apparent-size.


Basic Options
Option	Description
-a, --all	Show sizes for all files (not just directories)
-h, --human-readable	Print sizes in human-readable format (e.g., KB, MB, GB)
-s, --summarize	Display only a total summary for each argument
-c, --total	Show a grand total at the end
--apparent-size	Show apparent size (what ls shows) instead of disk usage
-k	Display sizes in kilobytes (KB) (default)
-m	Display sizes in megabytes (MB)
-g	Display sizes in gigabytes (GB)
-B, --block-size=SIZE	Scale sizes by SIZE (e.g., -BM = MB, -BG = GB)



Advanced Options
Option	Description
--max-depth=N	Limit output to directories up to N levels deep
--exclude=PATTERN	Exclude files/dirs matching PATTERN
--time	Show last modification time of files/dirs
--time=WORD	Show specific time (atime, ctime, mtime)
--files0-from=F	Read input filenames from file F (null-separated)
-l, --count-links	Count hard links multiple times (default: once)
-L, --dereference	Follow symbolic links (show target size)
-P, --no-dereference	Do not follow symlinks (default)
-x, --one-file-system	Skip directories on different filesystems
-0, --null	Separate output with NULL (for scripting)
-X, --exclude-from=FILE	Exclude files/dirs listed in FILE
-D, --dereference-args	Dereference only symlinks listed as arguments


Less Common Options
Option	Description
--inodes	Show inode usage instead of disk space
--threshold=SIZE	Exclude entries smaller than SIZE
--version	Display du version info
--help	Show help message


Show Disk Usage in MB, Limit Depth to 2

bash
du -hm --max-depth=2 /path/to/dir
Exclude Certain Files (e.g., .log)

bash
du -ah --exclude='*.log' /path/to/dir
Show Last Modified Time

bash
du -ah --time /path/to/dir
Only Show Directories > 1GB

bash
du -h --threshold=1G /path/to/dir
Read Input from a File (Null-Separated)

bash
du --files0-from=file_list.txt
